PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The requirements are equal voting control (via 50/50 voting shares + joint directorship), Alex compensated by salary (possible as an employee regardless of share rights), Tyler compensated by dividends (requires shares with dividend rights), and a mechanism to admit future non-voting investors.

Option A satisfies every point: Class A and Class B are identical voting dividend-bearing shares, so Tyler (50 A) and the Alex-wife corporation (50 B) give equal votes and board control; Alex can draw a salary as an employee; Tyler can receive dividends on his Class A shares; and Class C can be issued to outside investors as non-voting shares. The other choices each fail at least one requirement (reversed dividend rights, no non-voting class, or unequal board control).}

3.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

The requirements demand equal operational control (both as sole directors), different payment types (Alex via salary implying no dividend entitlement on his shares; Tyler via dividends), and the ability to admit outside investors on a strictly non-voting basis.

- A and D give both founders identical dividend rights, so they cannot segregate Tyler’s preferred dividend stream from Alex’s salary stream.  
- B reverses the dividend rights (Alex receives them, Tyler does not).  
- E adds Tyler’s sons to the board, destroying equal control, and therefore fails requirement 1.  

Only C assigns Alex non-dividend voting shares (Class A) and Tyler dividend-bearing voting shares (Class B) while keeping both as the sole directors. The non-voting Class C shares satisfy the “no-say” investor condition even though they carry no economic rights. Thus C is the only structure that meets every stated criterion simultaneously.}

